---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";

const toc = [
  { label: "Chats without a project", href: "#unscoped" },
  { label: "Code threads", href: "#code" },
  { label: "Work sessions", href: "#work" },
  { label: "Tasks board", href: "#kanban" },
  { label: "Right panel", href: "#surfaces" },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Threads and tasks"
  description="Start a chat with no folder open, keep repository work on a Code thread, and move tasks across Draft, In Progress, and Done."
  currentPath="/docs/threads"
  eyebrow="The workspace"
  {toc}
>
  <section id="unscoped">
    <h2>Chats without a project</h2>
    <p>
      On 1.0.0-alpha.1 RC you can start and send a chat without opening a Git
      repository. The landing screen opens a composer immediately. If you have
      never added a folder, that composer is unscoped: it talks to a provider
      you already signed in, but it is not bound to a working tree, branch, or
      worktree.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-callout">
      <strong>Work still needs a project.</strong>
      <p>
        Unscoped chats are for questions, planning, and throwaway turns. The
        cowork surface — Connections, artifacts, scheduled Work — still expects
        a folder. Open a repository when the next step is a diff or a pull
        request.
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      <code>mod+N</code> starts a new thread on the current project when one is
      selected. <code>mod+Shift+N</code> starts a new local thread for that
      project. See <a href="/docs/keybindings">Keyboard shortcuts</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="code">
    <h2>Code threads</h2>
    <p>
      A Code thread is a durable task on a Git project. It keeps the provider
      instance, permission mode, checkout (current tree or isolated worktree),
      transcript, diffs, and git actions together. Switching providers mid-thread
      starts a fresh session on the destination agent and replays context —
      create a <a href="/docs/checkpoints">checkpoint</a> first so the handoff
      is a seam, not a paste.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="work">
    <h2>Work sessions</h2>
    <p>
      Work is a separate cowork history. Sessions do not appear in Code’s chat
      list. Use it when the outcome is a document, a Connection, or research
      rather than a commit. Details: <a href="/docs/work">Work</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="kanban">
    <h2>Tasks board</h2>
    <p>
      Tasks is a Kanban of threads, not a second tracker. Columns are{" "}
      <strong>Draft</strong>, <strong>In Progress</strong>, and{" "}
      <strong>Done</strong>, derived from the thread’s real state. Open a card
      to jump to that thread. With no project selected, the board stacks every
      project so you can scan work across the workspace.
    </p>
    <p>
      Treat the board as a live status surface. Drag-to-dispatch (dropping a
      Draft card onto In Progress to send its prompt) is still landing; browsing
      and opening cards already work.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="surfaces">
    <h2>Right-panel surfaces</h2>
    <p>
      When a thread is open, the right panel is the environment: Changes,
      Browser, Terminal, Files, pull requests, agents, context, artifacts,
      Music, and Canvas. Letter shortcuts open a surface when you are not
      typing in the composer.
    </p>
    <div class="docs-table">
      <div><strong>B</strong><span>Browser — local app or URL.</span></div>
      <div><strong>M</strong><span>Music — Apple Music, Spotify, and other listening apps on this machine.</span></div>
      <div><strong>C</strong><span>Canvas — dashboards, slides, docs, spreadsheets.</span></div>
      <div><strong>T</strong><span>Terminal (Code threads).</span></div>
    </div>
    <p>
      Desktop-only extras — pop-out chat, WSL/SSH environments, the usage clock —
      live on <a href="/docs/desktop">Desktop &amp; environments</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
